          "label": "def:theory-completeness"
        }
      ],
      "gap": "Section 6.7 must develop consistency and the complete comparison of the distinct implications of soundness, completeness and consistency. The handbook route has the soundness and completeness definitions and the direction-check warning (inherited-00907-00984.tex:53-77) but, at this point, no general definition of consistency for a specified calculus and no statement with written proof of how the three notions bear on each other (for example: soundness makes every satisfiable set consistent and the calculus consistent; for an explosive calculus with negation, completeness is equivalent to every consistent set being satisfiable; consistency alone does not give soundness; consistency and deductive completeness of a theory differ from soundness and completeness of a calculus). Calculus-specific consistency definitions exist elsewhere (inherited-02545-02664.tex:4-7 for ND_CPL; inherited-04306-04575.tex:12-17 for ND_FOL; inherited-09498-09538.tex:4-12 for theories), and inherited-07150-07294.tex:60-80 and inherited-09498-09538.tex:14-19 contrast deductive with semantic completeness, but these are local to specific calculi or theories and do not constitute the general development and complete comparison owed in 6.7."
    }
